page("fishing-and-river-trip-grilling","Grilling on Fishing and River Trips","Pack the Slot Grill Compact for kayak, boat, sandbar and riverbank cooking: what fits, where to set up, cooking your catch, and leaving the bank clean.",IMG["c07"],f"""
<p>There's nothing like cooking on a sandbar or riverbank after a day on the water. The Slot Grill Compact packs to about 1" thick, so it slides into a kayak hatch, a boat storage compartment or the bottom of a dry bag, and it sets up in 30–60 seconds by hand. Because the 12-gauge carbon steel won't flex, it sits solid on sand and gravel, and it's strong enough to hold a cast-iron skillet of fish.</p>
<h2>Packing for the water</h2><ul><li><b>Grill flat-packed</b> in a bag or old box so soot doesn't get on your gear. Pack it low and centered in a kayak or boat.</li><li><b>Charcoal in a dry bag.</b> Water is the enemy; one splash ruins a bag of charcoal. Double-bag it.</li><li><b>Lighting kit in a waterproof container:</b> two lighters, newspaper or natural starters. A compact chimney helps.</li><li><b>Cooler</b> with ice for bait, catch and food (separate zones or separate coolers).</li><li><b>Griddle plate</b> (<a href="{GRID}">Slot Grill griddle</a>) for fish fillets, plus a wide spatula.</li><li>Tongs, gloves, thermometer, foil, oil, lemon, salt and pepper.</li><li>Metal ash can with lid, trash bags, water for putting out coals.</li></ul>
<h2>Where to set up</h2><ol><li>Check the rules: many rivers, lakes and public lands have their own fire rules and burn bans. Some require fires below the high-water line on sandbars; others ban them. Follow the land manager's rules.</li><li>Choose bare sand or gravel, away from dry driftwood piles, grass and boats.</li><li>Pick a spot sheltered from the wind if you can. Wind on open water and sandbars is often strong.</li><li>Level the sand under the grill so it sits flat.</li><li>Never use the grill on a boat deck or in a cabin.</li></ol>
<h2>Cooking your catch</h2><p>Where it's legal to keep fish, cooking them fresh is the best meal of the trip. See the full method in <a href="/grilled-fish-compact-grill.html">grilled fish on a small grill</a>; the short version:</p><ul><li>Keep catch on ice until cooking.</li><li>Clean and fillet away from camp, following local regulations for disposal of fish waste.</li><li>Dry fillets well, oil, and season.</li><li>Cook delicate fillets on the griddle or in foil, firm fish directly on the grate.</li><li>Cook to 145°F or until the flesh is opaque and flakes easily.</li></ul>
<h2>River trip menu ideas</h2><ul><li>Fresh fish with lemon and butter in foil</li><li><a href="/grilled-shrimp-compact-grill.html">Garlic shrimp</a> on skewers (from the cooler)</li><li><a href="/sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill.html">Sausages</a>: durable, easy, hold up in a cooler</li><li><a href="/campfire-breakfast-griddle.html">Griddle breakfast</a> on an overnight trip</li><li><a href="/smores-and-grilled-desserts-compact-grill.html">S'mores</a> on the last coals</li></ul>
<h2>Leave the bank clean</h2><ol><li>Let coals burn down. Drown with river water and stir until ash is cold to the touch, where allowed.</li><li>Pack ash out in a metal can with lid, or dispose of it as the rules require. Don't dump hot ash on sand; buried coals stay hot and burn bare feet.</li><li>Pack out all trash and food scraps.</li><li>Pack the grill flat once cool.</li></ol>
<h2>After the trip</h2><p>River and lake trips mean moisture. At home, wash off sand and grit, dry every piece completely, and re-oil. See <a href="/rust-prevention-carbon-steel-grill.html">rust prevention</a>.</p>
{SAFETY}
""","trips",[("Can I take a charcoal grill on a kayak?","The Slot Grill Compact packs to about 1 inch thick, so it fits a kayak hatch or dry bag. Cook only on shore, never on the boat."),("Can I build a fire on a river sandbar?","It depends on local rules. Some areas allow fires on sandbars below the high-water line and others ban them. Check with the land manager."),("How do I cook fresh fish on a small grill?","Dry and oil the fillets, cook delicate fish on a griddle or in foil and firm fish on the grate, to 145°F or until opaque and flaky."),("How do I keep charcoal dry on the water?","Double-bag it in dry bags and keep it in the center of the boat or kayak.")])

page("hunting-camp-cooking-compact-grill","Hunting Camp Cooking on a Compact Charcoal Grill","Cook at deer camp, duck camp and hunting leases on the Slot Grill Compact: camp setup, cooking backstrap and wild game, cold-weather fire tips and gear.",IMG["c28"],f"""
<p>Hunting camp cooking is usually done in the dark, in the cold, by tired people. That's exactly where a heavy-duty small grill shines. The Slot Grill Compact sets up in under a minute by hand, needs no tools or hinges, burns charcoal or cooking wood, and is made of 12-gauge carbon steel that doesn't care if it gets tossed in the truck bed. It packs about 1" flat, so it fits behind a seat or in the camp trailer.</p>
<h2>Camp setup</h2><ul><li>Set up on bare ground or gravel, clear of dry leaves, brush, tents, blinds and fuel cans.</li><li>Check lease, landowner and public land fire rules, including burn bans.</li><li>Keep a headlamp, gloves and water at the grill.</li><li>Keep the grill well away from where firearms, ammunition and fuel are stored.</li></ul>
<h2>Cooking wild game</h2><p>Venison, wild hog and waterfowl are lean (except hog), so they behave differently from store beef.</p><ul><li><b>Venison backstrap and tenderloin:</b> treat it like a thick steak. Oil it well, season, sear hard over a <a href="/two-zone-fire-on-a-small-grill.html">two-zone fire</a> and finish on the cool side. Many hunters like it rare to medium-rare; it dries out fast when overcooked. See the <a href="/reverse-sear-steak-small-grill.html">sear-and-slide method</a>.</li><li><b>Duck breast:</b> score the skin, start skin-side down on medium heat to render, then finish. Use the <a href="{GRID}">griddle</a> to catch the fat.</li><li><b>Wild hog:</b> cook thoroughly. Wild pork can carry parasites and diseases; follow wildlife agency guidance and use a thermometer.</li><li><b>Ground venison:</b> burgers and sausages cook to 160°F.</li><li><b>Field care matters most:</b> cool game quickly and keep it clean and cold.</li></ul>
<h2>Cold-weather fire tips</h2><ul><li>Light more charcoal than you would in summer; cold steel and air steal heat.</li><li>Keep charcoal dry in a lidded bin.</li><li>Shelter the grill from wind with safe clearance.</li><li>Keep coals tightly together and add lit coals early.</li><li>Expect longer cooks; trust the <a href="{THERMO}">thermometer</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>Camp menu ideas</h2><ul><li><a href="/campfire-breakfast-griddle.html">Pre-dawn breakfast</a>: bacon, eggs and potatoes on the griddle.</li><li>Backstrap with grilled onions.</li><li><a href="/sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill.html">Venison sausage and peppers</a>.</li><li><a href="/chicken-thighs-small-charcoal-grill.html">Chicken thighs</a> for the crew that didn't fill a tag.</li></ul>
<h2>Camp gear checklist</h2><ul><li>Slot Grill Compact and griddle</li><li>Chimney, charcoal, starters, lighters</li><li>Tongs, spatula, gloves, headlamp</li><li>Thermometer</li><li>Cooler(s), foil, oil, salt and pepper</li><li>Metal ash can</li></ul>
{SAFETY}
""","trips",[("Can I cook venison on a small charcoal grill?","Yes. Treat backstrap like a thick steak: sear over hot coals and finish on the cool side of a two-zone fire. It's lean and dries out quickly if overcooked."),("How do I keep a charcoal fire hot in cold weather?","Use more charcoal, keep it dry, keep coals together, shelter from wind, and add lit coals early."),("Does the Slot Grill Compact burn wood?","Yes. Slot Grill lists charcoal or cooking wood as fuel."),("What temperature should wild hog be cooked to?","Cook wild pork thoroughly and follow your wildlife agency's guidance; use a thermometer.")])
